Looking at this problem, I need to find the smallest $n$ such that $16!^2 \mid \prod_{1\le i<j\le n}(a_j-a_i)$ for every strictly increasing sequence of positive integers.

**Answer Leak Check:** The problem text contains no leaked answers or solution sketches. Proceeding.

## Proof

**Key tool: $p$-adic valuation of the Vandermonde product.** For a prime $p$, let $v_p$ denote $p$-adic valuation. For any set of $n$ distinct integers:

$$v_p\!\left(\prod_{i<j}(a_j-a_i)\right) = \sum_{k\ge 1} N_k$$

where $N_k = \#\{(i,j): i<j,\; a_i\equiv a_j \pmod{p^k}\}$. This holds because $v_p(a_j-a_i)=\sum_{k\ge 1}\mathbf{1}[p^k\mid(a_j-a_i)]$.

**Minimizing $N_k$.** Given $n$ integers distributed among $p^k$ residue classes, $N_k=\sum_c\binom{n_c}{2}$ where $n_c$ is the count in class $c$. This is minimized when the $n_c$ are as equal as possible. Writing $n=q\cdot p^k+r$, $0\le r<p^k$, the minimum is:

$$g(p^k,n)=r\binom{q+1}{2}+(p^k-r)\binom{q}{2}.$$

**The overall minimum is achievable.** Taking $a_i=i$ ($i=1,\dots,n$), consecutive integers distribute evenly modulo every $p^k$, achieving $N_k=g(p^k,n)$ at every level simultaneously. Thus:

$$\min_{a_1<\cdots<a_n} v_p\!\left(\prod_{i<j}(a_j-a_i)\right) = f_p(n):=\sum_{k\ge 1} g(p^k,n).$$

**Required thresholds.** Since $16! = \prod_p p^{v_p(16!)}$, we need $f_p(n)\ge 2\,v_p(16!)$ for each prime $p\mid 16!$. Computing:

| $p$ | $v_p(16!)$ | Required $2v_p(16!)$ | $f_p(n-1)$ | $f_p(n)$ | $n_p$ |
|-----|-----------|---------------------|------------|----------|-------|
| 2 | 15 | 30 | $f_2(9)=23$ | $f_2(10)=30$ | 10 |
| 3 | 6 | 12 | $f_3(9)=9$ | $f_3(10)=13$ | 10 |
| 5 | 3 | 6 | $f_5(10)=5$ | $f_5(11)=7$ | 11 |
| 7 | 2 | 4 | $f_7(10)=3$ | $f_7(11)=4$ | 11 |
| 11 | 1 | 2 | $f_{11}(12)=1$ | $f_{11}(13)=2$ | 13 |
| 13 | 1 | 2 | $f_{13}(14)=1$ | $f_{13}(15)=2$ | 15 |

**Detailed verification of key entries:**

*$p=2$, $n=10$:* $g(2,10)=2\binom{5}{2}=20$; $g(4,10)=2\binom{3}{2}+2\binom{2}{2}=8$; $g(8,10)=2\binom{2}{2}=2$; $g(16,10)=0$. Sum $=30\ge 30$. ✓

*$p=2$, $n=9$:* $g(2,9)=\binom{5}{2}+\binom{4}{2}=16$; $g(4,9)=\binom{3}{2}+3\binom{2}{2}=6$; $g(8,9)=\binom{2}{2}=1$. Sum $=23<30$. ✗

*$p=13$, $n=15$:* $g(13,15)=2\binom{2}{2}=2$; $g(169,15)=0$. Sum $=2\ge 2$. ✓

*$p=13$, $n=14$:* $g(13,14)=\binom{2}{2}=1$; $g(169,14)=0$. Sum $=1<2$. ✗

*$p=3$, $n=10$:* $g(3,10)=\binom{4}{2}+2\binom{3}{2}=12$; $g(9,10)=\binom{2}{2}=1$. Sum $=13\ge 12$. ✓

*$p=5$, $n=11$:* $g(5,11)=\binom{3}{2}+4\binom{2}{2}=7$; $g(25,11)=0$. Sum $=7\ge 6$. ✓

*$p=7$, $n=11$:* $g(7,11)=4\binom{2}{2}=4$; $g(49,11)=0$. Sum $=4\ge 4$. ✓

*$p=11$, $n=13$:* $g(11,13)=2\binom{2}{2}=2$; $g(121,13)=0$. Sum $=2\ge 2$. ✓

**$n=15$ suffices for all primes.** For $p=2$: $f_2(15)=49+21+7=77\ge 30$. For $p=3$: $f_3(15)=30+6=36\ge 12$. For $p=5$: $f_5(15)=15\ge 6$. For $p=7$: $f_7(15)=9\ge 4$. For $p=11$: $f_{11}(15)=4\ge 2$. For $p=13$: $f_{13}(15)=2\ge 2$. All satisfied.

**$n=14$ fails.** Take $a_i=i$ for $i=1,\dots,14$. Then $v_{13}\!\left(\prod_{i<j}(j-i)\right)=f_{13}(14)=1<2=2v_{13}(16!)$, so $13^2\nmid\prod_{i<j}(j-i)$, hence $16!^2\nmid\prod_{i<j}(j-i)$.

**Conclusion.** The smallest $n$ is:

$$\boxed{n=15}$$

